{ You will learn the fastest way to win a match. Four moves, and the game is over. Start with the pawn in the middle. [%cal Ye2e4] }
1. e4 { The pawn steps up and opens the way out for your queen and your bishop. [%csl Gd1,Gf1] } (1. d4 { A fine opening, but it leads to a different kind of game with no quick mate. } 1... d5 2. c4 { This is the queens gambit. Good chess, and nobody gets mated in four moves. }) (1. Nf3 { A good developing move, but your knight now blocks your own queen. [%csl Rf3] } 1... d5 2. d4 { Solid chess, and the four move mate is no longer possible. }) 1... e5 { He copies you. Bring your bishop out to aim at the red square, the weak one next to his king. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yf1c4] } 2. Bc4 { Your bishop aims at the red square, and only his king is guarding it. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gc4f7] } (2. Qh5 { The queen can come out first too. But she is in the open early, where pieces can chase her. } 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 { The same idea, one move later. }) (2. Nf3 { This leads to the Italian, a fine opening. But your knight blocks your queen. [%csl Rf3] } 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 { Good chess, though the quick mate is gone. }) (2. Qf3 { Your queen aims at the red square from here as well, but his knight can block her. [%csl Rf7] } 2... Nf6 { The knight steps in the way and the attack is over. [%csl Gf6] }) 2... Nc6 { He defends his pawn. Now bring your queen out to aim at the red square as well. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yd1h5] } 3. Qh5 { We bring the queen out for a quick attack. Risky against an expert, effective against a beginner, who may answer with a natural knight move. [%cal Gh5f7,Gc4f7] } (3. Qf3 { She aims at the red square from this side too, but now he has an easy block. [%csl Rf7] } 3... Nf6 { His knight covers everything and you have nothing left. [%csl Gf6] }) (3. Nf3 { Sensible chess. Now you are simply playing the Italian opening. } 3... Bc5 { A good game for both sides, but your queen never came out. }) (3. Bxf7+ { Your bishop cannot do this alone. He simply takes it. } 3... Kxf7 { You gave a bishop for one pawn and his king walks away safely. }) (3. Nc3 { A normal developing move, but it does nothing about the red square. [%csl Rf7] } 3... Nf6 { Solid chess for both. The trap needed your queen out first. [%cal Gd1h5] }) (3. d3 { Quiet and sensible, but your queen is still sitting at home. } 3... Nf6 { A perfectly good game, and nothing like a four move mate. }) 3... Nf6 { Your opponent made a mistake. Find the checkmate and finish the game. } 4. Qxf7# { Mate! Your queen lands right next to his king and your bishop protects her. That is scholars mate. [%csl Gf7][%cal Gc4f7] } (4. Bxf7+ { Check, but it throws the mate away: his knight is attacking your queen. [%cal Rf6h5] } 4... Ke7 5. Qd1 Kxf7 { Your queen ran home and he won your bishop. All that for nothing. }) (4. Qxe5+ { A check and a free pawn, but his knight was guarding that square. [%cal Rc6e5] } 4... Nxe5 { You lost your queen for one pawn, and the mate that was there is gone. }) 1-0


