{ Now you are black, and one day someone will try this on you. Do not panic. }
3. Qh5 { His queen comes out. She and his bishop both attack the red square, so push your g pawn and chase her away. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yg7g6] } 3... g6 { The pawn blocks her path and attacks her at the same time. She has to move again. [%cal Gg6h5] } (3... Nf6 { The most natural move on the board, and it walks straight into mate. } 4. Qxf7# { His queen lands beside your king, his bishop protects her, and you have no escape. }) (3... d6 { It looks solid, but it does nothing about the red square. [%csl Rf7] } 4. Qxf7# { The same mate. Guarding the middle does not guard the square beside your king. }) (3... Qe7 { Good defending: your queen now guards the red square and the mate is dead. [%csl Rf7][%cal Ge7f7] } 4. d3 Nf6 { Perfectly fine, though your queen is standing in your own bishops way. }) (3... Qf6 { Your queen guards the red square too, but she has taken the square your knight wanted. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gf6f7] } 4. Nc3 Bc5 { Playable, and a little clumsy. The pawn push is the tidy answer. }) (3... Nh6 { This knight guards the red square, but it is stuck out on the edge of the board. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gh6f7] } 4. Nc3 g6 { You play the pawn push anyway, with an unhappy knight. }) (3... d5 { It attacks his bishop, and it costs you a pawn and then a knight. } 4. Bxd5 Qf6 5. Bxc6+ bxc6 { He is a pawn up and your queen side is wrecked. Not worth it. }) 4. Qf3 { His queen slides across and attacks the red square again. Block her path with your knight. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yg8f6] } 4... Nf6 { Your knight blocks her path. He has moved his queen twice while you brought out two pieces, so you are better. [%csl Gf6] } (4... Bg7 { A lovely square for the bishop, but her path to the red square is still open. [%csl Rf7][%cal Rf3f7] } 5. Qxf7# { Mate. The knight had to block first; the bishop could have waited. }) (4... d6 { It looks solid, but that pawn does not block her path at all. } 5. Qxf7# { Mate again. She came straight down the column, so that is the path to block. }) (4... Qe7 { Your queen guards the red square, and that works perfectly well too. [%csl Rf7][%cal Ge7f7] } 5. Ne2 Nf6 { Fine chess. The knight does the same job and stays out of the way. }) (4... Qf6 { You offer a queen trade. She guards the red square, but she takes your knights best square. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gf6f7] } 5. Qxf6 Nxf6 { Queens come off and the danger is over. Fine, though the pawn push was tidier. }) (4... Nh6 { It stops the mate, but the knight is miserable out on the edge. } 5. Ne2 Na5 { Playable. The other knight was the one that wanted this job. }) (4... d5 { It attacks his bishop, but he simply takes it and keeps the threat. } 5. Bxd5 Qd7 { A pawn gone, and you are still busy defending the red square. [%csl Rf7] }) 0-1


