{ The mate is dead. Now make him pay for it, because his queen is still out in the open. }
5. Nc3 { He finally develops a piece, but his queen is still stuck out in the middle. Can you attack her and gain time? [%csl Rf3] } 5... Nd4 { Your knight lands in the middle and attacks two things at once: his queen and a loose pawn. [%cal Gd4f3,Gd4c2] } (5... Bg7 { A good square for the bishop, but it lets his queen walk away for free. } 6. Nge2 d6 { A fine game. But you had a free attack on his queen and let it go. }) (5... d6 { Solid, and slow. His queen was the one you wanted to chase. } 6. Nd5 Bg7 { Playable, and now his knight is the annoying one. }) (5... Nb4 { The other jump. This knight attacks a pawn, but it lets his queen off the hook. [%cal Gb4c2] } 6. Bb3 { His bishop steps back and guards that pawn, so your knight has no job to do. [%cal Gb3c2] }) 6. Qd1 { All the way home. He has spent three moves on his queen and she is back where she began. Keep bringing pieces out. } 6... Bg7 { Your bishop takes the long diagonal. He spent three moves on his queen; you spent yours developing. [%cal Gg7a1] } (6... Nxc2+ { A free pawn, and a fork of his king and rook! But his queen has just come home. } 7. Qxc2 { She guards that square from home, and your whole knight is gone. [%cal Rd1c2] }) (6... c6 { Just as good: the pawn takes the best square away from his knight. } 7. Nf3 Bg7 { Excellent. Your bishop comes out anyway, one move later. }) (6... Bc5 { A good square too, but your g pawn opened a better road for this bishop. } 7. Nf3 c6 { Perfectly playable, just a little less to do with your plan. }) (6... d6 { Safe and slow. Your bishop was already asking for the road your g pawn opened. } 7. d3 { Fine for both, but your bishop wanted the green square. [%csl Gg7] }) 0-1
